      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": "18 patients suffering from primary fibromyalgia received nine training sessions using EMG biofeedback over a period of four weeks. Pre- and posttreatment measurement of the baseline EMG activity of the trapezius, muscular sensitivity, and cognitive variables (helplessness and belief of control) were taken. Analysis indicated a significant reduction occurred in general intensity of pain and in EMG activity as well as a significant increase in muscular sensitivity. Multiple regression analyses indicated that the increase in muscular sensitivity correlated with the decrease of EMG activity in the trapezius baseline. Self-reported pain reduction was predicted by a change in cognitive variables."},
